.page hwclock 8 show or set the battery-backed hardware clock

# SYNOPSIS
*hwclock* [*-s* | *-w*] [*--localtime* | *--utc*]

# DESCRIPTION
`hwclock` talks to the clock chip of the machine, the one that keeps time while the power is off. Without options it shows the time the chip holds, as `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` followed by the offset from UTC.

The chip normally keeps UTC. A machine that also runs another system may keep local time in it instead; `--localtime` tells `hwclock` to read and write the chip that way.

# OPTIONS
@ *-s*, *--hctosys*
    Set the system clock from the chip.
@ *-w*, *--systohc*
    Write the system clock to the chip.
@ *--localtime*
    The chip holds local time.
@ *--utc*
    The chip holds UTC (the default).

# EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if the chip could not be read or a clock could not be set; 2 for a usage error.

# EXAMPLES
    hwclock
    hwclock --localtime -s

# SEE ALSO
date(1), uptime(1)
